;; RM-B2: one name, two sorts -- rejected, never guessed.
;;
;; `ready` resolves to no function at all, which the predicate language calls
;; an ABSTRACT measure: an uninterpreted mathematical function with no
;; declaration to read a sort from.  Position decides instead -- Bool where a
;; proposition is required, Int everywhere else -- and here the two positions
;; disagree: `:pre` uses it as a proposition, the return refinement uses it as
;; a number.
;;
;; Picking one would declare a symbol that means one thing in the hypotheses
;; and another in the goal.  The hash-cons table keys off the declaration, so
;; those are two symbols that PRINT the same, which is precisely how a
;; congruence bug gets written.  Rejecting is the correct answer: the
;; obligation falls to Unknown with a stated reason and the runtime check the
;; predicate would have had anyway is kept.  --strict-refine turns that
;; Unknown into an error, which is what makes this fixture negative.
;;
;; The second diagnostic ("unknown function or operator 'ready'") is the
;; ordinary language rule and is expected: a check the compiler keeps must be
;; a check it can EVALUATE, and a measure with no definition cannot back one.
;; The two rejections are independent -- the refinement layer must not depend
;; on the elaborator having caught this.
(defn f [v : int] : #refine{ r : int | (< (ready r) 3) }
  :pre (ready v)
  v)

(defn main [] : int
  (println (f 1))
  0)
